  "plan": {
    "goal": "Measure intrinsic hepatic metabolic capacity using antipyrine (low-extraction, capacity-limited probe) in a perfused liver-on-chip, targeting a low-extraction regime (E ≈ 0.15) at 2 µL/min perfusion.",
    "rationale": "Antipyrine is the canonical low-extraction (E < 0.3) probe: its clearance is capacity-limited and reflects intrinsic enzyme activity rather than perfusion (Rowland & Tozer). I set a target extraction ratio of 0.15 (inlet 10 µM → outlet 8.5 µM), firmly in the low-extraction regime, so the measured clearance reports metabolic capacity. At Q = 2 µL/min, Cl = E·Q = 0.30 µL/min. HepG2 (immortalized hepatoma, BSL-1) seeded at 1e5 cells/cm² on a 400×100 µm × 20 mm channel (0.08 cm²) gives 8000 cells. The 2 µL/min flow produces 0.05 Pa wall shear (bottom of the HepG2 physiological 0.05–0.15 Pa range), Re = 0.13 (laminar), residence time 24 s, and O2 supply ≈ 21× demand (no hypoxia). A cell-free (blank) chip control is essential to subtract PDMS/tubing binding from cell-mediated clearance.",
    "chip": {
      "width_um": 400.0,
      "height_um": 100.0,
      "length_mm": 20.0,
      "channel_count": 1,
      "material": "PDMS"
    },
